## filter

`tabula filter FILE CONDITION...` prints the rows where every CONDITION holds, in the order of the file, with the same
columns. A CONDITION is one argument shaped `COLUMN OP VALUE` with no spaces added (`price>=10`, `city==Leeds`,
`name~gnome`). The operators are `==`, `!=`, `>=`, `<=`, `>`, `<` and `~` (the cell contains VALUE, upper and lower case
ignored, as the `icontains` transform does). The operator is the first one that appears in the argument, so a column name
cannot contain one; where two start at the same place the longer wins (`>=`, not `>`). VALUE may be empty (`city==`
matches the empty cells). For every operator but `~`, when both the cell and VALUE are numbers they are compared as exact
decimals (`10` is greater than `9`, `1.0` equals `1`); otherwise they are compared as text, by code point. A table with
no row left still prints its header. A column the file does not have is a mistake that names the column. An argument
with no operator, one with nothing before the operator and a missing CONDITION are usage errors, found before the file
is read.

## group

`tabula group FILE BY AGGREGATE...` prints one row per distinct value of column BY, in the order each value first
appears in the file, with the columns BY and then one per AGGREGATE, named as written with `:` replaced by `_`
(`sum:qty` gives `sum_qty`). An AGGREGATE is `count` (the rows in the group) or `sum:COLUMN`, `min:COLUMN`,
`max:COLUMN` or `mean:COLUMN` over the numbers of that column. Everything is exact decimal arithmetic. `sum`, `min` and
`max` print as plain numbers, without a `+` sign or an exponent, and keep their decimals (`10.50` stays `10.50`).
`mean` is the exact sum divided by the count, rounded half up to two decimals and always printed with two (`5.00`,
`2.25`). A cell that is not a number in a column being summed, compared or averaged is a mistake that names the file,
the row and the column, as in `sort` (`t.csv: row 2: column 'price': 'abc' is not a number`); the row counts from 1 in
the order of the file and the lowest bad row is the one named. `count` reads no cell. A column the file does not have is
a mistake that names the column, and so is a result column named like BY (`sum:x` over a BY column called `sum_x`).
A table with no rows prints only its header. At least one AGGREGATE is needed; an unknown one (`avg:price`, `count:x`,
`sum` with no column) or the same one twice is a usage error, found before the file is read.

## join

`tabula join LEFT RIGHT KEY` joins two files on the column KEY, which both must have. The result has the columns of
LEFT, then the columns of RIGHT except KEY. A RIGHT column whose name LEFT already has gets `_right` added to its name
(`name` becomes `name_right`); if that still leaves two columns of the same name the result cannot be printed, which is
a mistake (`join: the result would have column 'name_right' twice`). Values of KEY match when their text is exactly
equal (`1` and `01` do not match). With `--kind inner` (the default) the rows are, for each LEFT row in the order of
LEFT, one row per matching RIGHT row in the order of RIGHT; a LEFT row with no match is left out. With `--kind left` a
LEFT row with no match appears once, with empty text in the RIGHT columns. A KEY that either file does not have is a
mistake that names the file and the column. Exactly three arguments are needed; anything else is a usage error, found
before either file is read. `--kind` takes `inner` or `left`; any other value is a usage error.

